## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem tests dynamic programming with turn constraints on grid paths, requiring careful state design and path reconstruction. It evaluates understanding of edge case handling and performance optimization for large inputs.

## Key Concepts

- **4D DP state**: position (i, j), turn count (t), and ending direction (d)
- **Two transition types**: continuing in the same direction versus making a turn
- **Path reconstruction**: using parent pointers or backtracking to retrieve the actual path
- **Time and space trade-offs**: consider optimization for large grids

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPathWithKTurns(M, k) {
  const n = M.length;
  const m = M[0].length;
  const dp = Array(n).fill(null).map(() =>
    Array(m).fill(null).map(() =>
      Array(k + 1).fill(null).map(() => [-1, -1])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = dp[0][0][0][1] = M[0][0];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        for (let d = 0; d < 2; d++) {
          if (dp[i][j][t][d] < 0) continue;

          if (i + 1 < n) {
            const newD = 1;
            const newT = d === newD ? t : t + 1;
            if (newT <= k) {
              dp[i + 1][j][newT][newD] = Math.max(
                dp[i + 1][j][newT][newD],
                dp[i][j][t][d] + M[i + 1][j]
              );
            }
          }

          if (j + 1 < m) {
            const newD = 0;
            const newT = d === newD ? t : t + 1;
            if (newT <= k) {
              dp[i][j + 1][newT][newD] = Math.max(
                dp[i][j + 1][newT][newD],
                dp[i][j][t][d] + M[i][j + 1]
              );
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return Math.max(dp[n - 1][m - 1][k][0], dp[n - 1][m - 1][k][1]);
}
```

## Complexity

- **Time**: O(n × m × k)
- **Space**: O(n × m × k)

## Why This Is Asked
Combinatorial DP with partitioning; tests clear recurrence and reconstruction, common in interviews. 

## Key Concepts
- dp[i][t] = min over p of dp[p][t-1] + max(A[p..i-1]);
- Track parent to reconstruct partition points; iterate i and t in increasing order.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = Math.max(...A.slice(0,i));
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      let curMax = -Infinity;
      for(let p=i-1; p>=t-1; p--){
        curMax = Math.max(curMax, A[p]);
        const cand = dp[p][t-1] + curMax;
        if(cand < dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = cand; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {cost: dp[n][k], par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time using a deque-based technique when max is a sliding property? 
- How would you extend to allow hypothetical negative numbers or to reconstruct explicit subarrays?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP on partitioning with a minimax objective, a practical budgeting scenario.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions; - Recurrence: min over j of max of subproblem and block sum; - Reconstruction via parent pointers.

## Code Example
```javascript
// sketch
function partitionMinMaxSum(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+a[i];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n},()=>new Array(k+1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n},()=>new Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][1]=pref[i+1];
  for (let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for (let i=0;i<n;i++){
      for (let j=0;j<i;j++){
        const val = Math.max(dp[j][t-1], pref[i+1]-pref[j+0]);
        if (val<dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; par[i][t]=j; }
      }
    }
  }
  return {best: dp[n-1][Math.min(k,n)], cuts: par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement a monotone-queue or divide-and-conquer optimization here?
- How would you adapt for exactly k vs at most k blocks and output exact cut points?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP setup for segment selection with non-adjacency and reconstruction, a common beginner DP pattern with a twist.

## Key Concepts
- DP over index i and blocks t
- Enforce non-adjacency via s-2 reference
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- Reconstruction pointers for endpoints
- Time/space trade-offs; possible optimization to O(nk) time and O(n) space per k

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarraysSum(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) P[i + 1] = P[i] + A[i];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i - 1][t]);
      if (t > 0) {
        for (let s = 1; s <= i; s++) {
          const sum = P[i] - P[s - 1];
          const prev = (s >= 2) ? dp[s - 2][t - 1] : (t - 1 === 0 ? 0 : -Infinity);
          const cand = sum + prev;
          if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = cand;
            take[i][t] = s;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would trace back using 'take' from (n, bestT)
  return { maxSum: dp[n][0], blocks: [] };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests formulating a DP over partitions with a subarray-internal metric.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP
- Prefix sums
- Reconstruction pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
// DP skeleton
function maxValue(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i]+A[i];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const cut = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) {
     for (let t=1;t<=Math.min(i,k);t++) {
        let maxVal = -Infinity; let bestP = -1; let curMax = -Infinity;
        for (let p=i-1; p>=t-1; p--) {
           curMax = Math.max(curMax, A[p]);
           const val = dp[p][t-1] + (pref[i]-pref[p]) - curMax;
           if (val>maxVal) { maxVal=val; bestP=p; }
        }
        dp[i][t] = maxVal; cut[i][t] = bestP;
     }
  }
  return {value: dp[n][k], cuts: cut};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- What is the time complexity and how would you optimize to O(nk) with a deque?
- How would you reconstruct the exact subarray endpoints?

## Why This Is Asked
- Tests DP state design that tracks both position and budget (flips).
- Requires reconstruction to return exact indices, not just value.

## Key Concepts
- DP state with dual dimension (i, flips)
- Transition depends on current bit; reconstruction via predecessor pointers
- Time/space trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxOnesRun(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: k+1}, () => null));
  let bestLen = 0, bestEnd = 0, bestJ = 0;
  for (let i=0; i<n; i++) {
    for (let j=0; j<=k; j++) {
      if (s[i] === '1') {
        dp[i][j] = (i>0 ? dp[i-1][j] : 0) + 1;
        prev[i][j] = i>0 ? [i-1, j] : null;
      } else if (j>0) {
        dp[i][j] = (i>0 ? dp[i-1][j-1] : 0) + 1;
        prev[i][j] = i>0 ? [i-1, j-1] : null;
      } else {
        dp[i][j] = 0; prev[i][j] = null;
      }
      if (dp[i][j] > bestLen) { bestLen = dp[i][j]; bestEnd = i; bestJ = j; }
    }
  }
  const start = bestEnd - bestLen + 2;
  return {bestLen, start, end: bestEnd+1, flips: bestJ};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reduce space to O(k)?
- How would you adapt if flips must happen in blocks?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced tree DP with a connectivity constraint and a knapsack-like merge across children. Requires careful reconstruction of the chosen nodes.

## Key Concepts

- Tree DP: dp[u][s] for connected subgraphs containing u within its subtree
- Knapsack-like merging of children to combine subtree sizes
- Path reconstruction: storing decisions to recover node set
- Complexity: O(nk) time and O(nk) space; handle negative values with offset

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxConnectedSubtreeSum(n, k, edges, val) {
  const g = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { g[u].push(v); g[v].push(u); }
  const parent = Array(n).fill(-1);
  const order = [];
  function dfs(u, p) {
    parent[u] = p;
    for (const v of g[u]) if (v !== p) dfs(v, u);
    order.push(u);
  }
  dfs(0, -1);
  // dp[u] will be an array of length min(subtreeSize[u], k) + 1
  // Initialization, merging and reconstruction would follow a standard tree knapsack pattern
  // Full implementation is lengthy; outline focuses on the recurrence above
  return { best: null, nodes: [] };
}
```

